    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, I can certainly help you summarize the key arguments for originalism in constitutional law. Proponents of originalism generally argue that the Constitution must be interpreted according to the original understanding of the text at the time it was adopted to preserve democratic legitimacy and limit judicial overreach. This view emphasizes that the framers and ratifiers intended the word \"The People\" to mean actual political sovereignty during the founding era, ensuring that the government remains accountable to the people's explicit consent.\nAdditionally, originalists contend that other methods of interpretation, like abandoning established meaning, lead to underenforcement of the text and allow unelected judges to impose new policies under the guise of the law.",
      "source": "target",
