    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that's a good summary. i've heard some critics say that originalism is basically just a way for judges to push a specific political agenda while pretending they're being neutral. how do scholars respond to the idea that it's more of a political tool than a real legal theory?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Scholars responding to this criticism argue that originalism is designed specifically to constrain political power by binding judges to the public law they were elected to uphold, rather than their personal policies. They contend that while the *application* of the theory may have political consequences in specific cases, the method itself aims for neutrality by prioritizing the democratic will of the past over an unelected judge's preferred outcome. Furthermore, many defenders point out that critics often misinterpret \"original intent\" (which is often hard to discern) as \"current liberal politics,\" whereas true originalism uses \"original public meaning\" to objectively reference the common understanding of the text at the time of its adoption.",
      "source": "target",
