    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i see. so it's basically a debate about whether it's a real constraint or just a mask. but if originalism is so focused on the past, how do scholars justify using it for modern issues that the founders couldn't have possibly imagined, like the internet or modern privacy rights? isn't it kind of problematic to apply 18th century logic to 21st century problems?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Scholars argue that originalism relies on the \"counterfactual\" question of whether the founders would agree with modern conclusions if they knew the technology, rather than the \"hypothetical\" fact that they imagined the specific technology. They justify this by asserting that the \"meaning\" of a word like \"liberty\" or \"overall power\" must be established at the time of writing to be neutral, preventing judges from inserting their own contemporary values under the guise of liberty.\nConsequently, the originalist approach insists that if a 19th-century legislature explicitly repealed a right, that right is gone for sure today to protect the integrity of the authorizing will, even if we would support the right now for different reasons.",
      "source": "target",
